00:02:11.914 --> 00:02:13.682
So an Arrhenius acid-base--

00:02:13.682 --> 00:02:14.783
OH, H plus.

00:02:14.783 --> 00:02:16.752
Bronsted-Lowry.

00:02:16.752 --> 00:02:19.488
It's proton transfer.

00:02:19.588 --> 00:02:21.390
And then there's
Lewis, who broasted

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:25.961
these guys, because
he said, uh-uh,

00:02:25.961 --> 00:02:30.199
and this is the most general way
to think about acids and bases.

00:02:30.199 --> 00:02:31.400
It is the most general.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:39.675
It is quite powerful, because
you go back to say this one,

00:02:39.675 --> 00:02:42.811
and so if this is a--

00:02:42.811 --> 00:02:46.815
what if we mix this with this?

00:02:46.815 --> 00:02:49.051
So here we have H.

00:02:49.051 --> 00:02:51.954
Now, remember, Lewis had all
the dots all over the place.

00:02:51.954 --> 00:02:55.424
He loved dots, and so
they're all over the place.

00:02:55.424 --> 00:03:00.195
And that's what if you had
this, this is a Lewis acid,

00:03:00.195 --> 00:03:04.733
because it is ready to receive.

00:03:04.733 --> 00:03:06.435
What is it ready to receive?

00:03:06.435 --> 00:03:09.505
No more atoms, only electrons.

00:03:09.505 --> 00:03:12.641
It is the most general
way to think about it.

00:03:12.641 --> 00:03:13.742
All right?

00:03:13.742 --> 00:03:17.246
And this would be a
Lewis base, because it

00:03:17.246 --> 00:03:21.350
is ready to give a
pair of electrons

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:22.851
and form a bond together.

00:03:22.851 --> 00:03:26.588
And that's what they do,
or that's what they can do.

00:03:26.588 --> 00:03:31.293
So a Lewis acid and a Lewis
base is the most general form,

00:03:31.293 --> 00:03:32.795
and you really get rid of--

00:03:32.795 --> 00:03:37.266
so for Lewis, it's all
about electron transfer,

00:03:37.266 --> 00:03:43.204
electron pair transfer, pair
transfer, or bonds if you're--

00:03:43.204 --> 00:03:45.974
I'm just going to make it
as general as possible.

00:03:45.974 --> 00:03:47.910
If you're going to mix
an acid and a base,

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:50.779
which I'll do in a
second, it's a pair.

00:03:50.779 --> 00:03:54.650
Electron transfer is
what it's all about,

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:57.219
and what happens here?

00:03:57.219 --> 00:04:00.556
Well, you don't have
any chemical identity,

00:04:00.556 --> 00:04:02.491
no more chemical identity.

00:04:05.861 --> 00:04:06.595
So it's full.

00:04:06.595 --> 00:04:08.163
You don't need the
hydrogens anymore.

00:04:08.163 --> 00:04:09.898
You don't need the OH's.

00:04:09.898 --> 00:04:11.767
It's totally general.

00:04:11.767 --> 00:04:14.103
And this really opens
it up, because now you

00:04:14.103 --> 00:04:17.206
can look at gases, liquid,
solids, all forms of matter,

00:04:17.206 --> 00:04:19.641
all elements in the table, and
you can think about them as

00:04:19.641 --> 00:04:21.810
whether they're going
to have an acid-like

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:24.780
or a base-like character in
terms of electron transfer.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.451
So here's the general-- if you
had a Lewis acid and a Lewis

00:04:29.451 --> 00:04:32.454
base, and you mix them together.

00:04:32.454 --> 00:04:36.358
Then, you can see that
if you got an electron

00:04:36.358 --> 00:04:37.659
pair that could then go--

00:04:37.659 --> 00:04:39.895
that one of them wants it,
and the other can give it,

00:04:39.895 --> 00:04:42.464
and they can form
a bond, then that

00:04:42.464 --> 00:04:45.267
would be how you think about
mixing a Lewis acid and a Lewis

00:04:45.267 --> 00:04:45.768
base.

00:04:45.768 --> 00:04:48.971
And again, this
won't be on the exam,

00:04:48.971 --> 00:04:52.707
but I just had to
give Lewis his due.

00:04:52.707 --> 00:04:57.913
OK, Lewis acids and bases
are all about the electrons.

