me, a peak and a number, 35.5.

00:13:59.888 --> 00:14:00.388
Right?

00:14:00.388 --> 00:14:04.592
That's the 2 theta
value for the 311 peak.

00:14:04.592 --> 00:14:06.695
Well, I think I'll
take that as a hint.

00:14:06.695 --> 00:14:10.198
Maybe I should think about that
number, and OK, so let's see.

00:14:10.198 --> 00:14:15.103
From Bragg, so from
Bragg, you know

00:14:15.103 --> 00:14:23.778
that you got n lambda
equals 2d sine theta,

00:14:23.778 --> 00:14:26.881
and remember that had to do with
constructive and destructive

00:14:26.881 --> 00:14:29.918
interference of waves, the
waves being the X-rays.

00:14:29.918 --> 00:14:35.023
And then we said, well, n is 1
in our class, so that's just 1

00:14:35.023 --> 00:14:35.757
in 3,091.

00:14:35.757 --> 00:14:36.958
It doesn't have to be.

00:14:36.958 --> 00:14:40.028
You could have interference
off of two planes down,

00:14:40.028 --> 00:14:41.563
three planes down,
but in this class,

00:14:41.563 --> 00:14:43.698
we're just doing the two
planes next to each other.

00:14:43.698 --> 00:14:44.833
So n is 1.

00:14:44.833 --> 00:14:46.301
OK?

00:14:46.301 --> 00:14:50.138
Now, I'll take that angle, and
I'll round it to 18 degrees.

00:14:50.138 --> 00:14:56.211
So I'm going to say for theta
equals 18 degrees, then--

00:14:56.211 --> 00:14:59.147
oh, wait, why is it 18?

00:14:59.147 --> 00:15:00.949
Because that's 2 theta.

00:15:00.949 --> 00:15:01.449
Right?

00:15:01.449 --> 00:15:04.018
Because remember, we
take spectra in 2 theta,

00:15:04.018 --> 00:15:06.354
but the theta in
Bragg is 1 theta.

00:15:06.354 --> 00:15:09.257
OK, that wasn't too hard.

00:15:09.257 --> 00:15:16.431
But now, I've got, let's see,
sine theta is around 0.3,

00:15:16.431 --> 00:15:19.734
and oh, I got lambda.

00:15:19.734 --> 00:15:27.008
Lambda equals 1.54 nanometers,
and so I can actually

00:15:27.008 --> 00:15:29.010
plug in now.

00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:31.012
I didn't even need the peak.

00:15:31.012 --> 00:15:37.385
I'm just plugging in the Bragg
condition, and I've got d.

00:15:37.385 --> 00:15:43.324
d equals 2.52
Angstroms, so I'm done.

00:15:43.324 --> 00:15:44.559
No.

00:15:44.559 --> 00:15:46.895
No, I'm not done, because
the question says,

00:15:46.895 --> 00:15:49.164
what's the lattice constant?

00:15:49.164 --> 00:15:50.331
What's the lattice constant?

00:15:50.331 --> 00:15:53.334
This d, I didn't say--

00:15:53.334 --> 00:15:54.536
I did something very bad--

00:15:54.536 --> 00:15:57.172
I didn't say what d--

00:15:57.172 --> 00:15:59.841
d is in hkl.

00:15:59.841 --> 00:16:03.711
That's what an XRD does.

00:16:03.711 --> 00:16:07.782
XRD, that's a different
than diffraction, but the d,

00:16:07.782 --> 00:16:11.986
in Bragg, is the distance
between the mirror planes

00:16:11.986 --> 00:16:13.822
that are scattering.

00:16:13.822 --> 00:16:14.456
All right?

00:16:14.456 --> 00:16:18.293
And so that's some
cuts in the crystal.

00:16:18.293 --> 00:16:23.131
That's some hkl, and
we know from that era

00:16:23.131 --> 00:16:27.335
that there is an equation
for cubic crystals

00:16:27.335 --> 00:16:30.805
that gives us hkl
which is a divided

00:16:30.805 --> 00:16:38.813
by the square root of h squared
plus k squared plus l squared.

00:16:38.813 --> 00:16:46.387
And that is the lattice
constant of the crystal,

00:16:46.387 --> 00:16:49.724
and that would be the
distance between any planes,

00:16:49.724 --> 00:16:51.893
any set of planes,
in the crystal,

00:16:51.893 --> 00:16:53.194
and that's what we need.

00:16:53.194 --> 00:16:58.933
Now, I can use that,
combine it with the d,

00:16:58.933 --> 00:17:03.938
and I can get that the lattice
constant is 8.38 Angstroms.

00:17:03.938 --> 00:17:05.406
Good.

00:17:05.406 --> 00:17:08.041
But the second question is--

00:17:08.041 --> 00:17:10.744
you don't even need to do
any math to answer that--

00:17:10.744 --> 00:17:13.515
what is the lattice structure?

00:17:13.515 --> 00:17:16.684
Here, we simply go to
our selection rules,

00:17:16.684 --> 00:17:23.925
and we know from before if
they're all even or all odd--

00:17:23.925 --> 00:17:25.792
so here we have, let's
see, all these peaks--

00:17:25.792 --> 00:17:29.697
220, 311, 400.

00:17:29.697 --> 00:17:31.332
You can see, they're
always all even,

00:17:31.332 --> 00:17:32.634
or they're always all odd.

00:17:32.634 --> 00:17:35.203
From our selection rules,
what kind of crystal is that?

00:17:38.006 --> 00:17:40.208
It's got to be FCC.

00:17:40.208 --> 00:17:41.042
All right?

00:17:41.042 --> 00:17:42.810
It's got to be FCC.

00:17:42.810 --> 00:17:45.914
So that's the answer
to that question,

00:17:45.914 --> 00:17:48.182
just from looking at
the peaks that you get

00:17:48.182 --> 00:17:51.819
and matching them with the
selection rules that you know,

00:17:51.819 --> 00:17:53.154
that we've already talked about.

00:17:55.823 --> 00:17:58.826
You could also, if you
didn't have the planes--

00:17:58.826 --> 00:18:01.996
so this is an example, where
you're given all these planes.

00:18:01.996 --> 00:18:04.299
You're given all these hkls,
but what if instead it's

00:18:04.299 --> 00:18:06.367
like the one we had in class?

00:18:06.367 --> 00:18:07.602
We went through this in class.

00:18:07.602 --> 00:18:08.903
Here, I'm not given the planes.

00:18:08.903 --> 00:18:13.641
I'm given instead
just two theta values.

00:18:13.641 --> 00:18:17.912
Well now, you've got to go
and use the procedure of XRD,

00:18:17.912 --> 00:18:19.380
and I'm not going
to do that again.

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:21.983
We've already done
that pretty thoroughly,

00:18:21.983 --> 00:18:24.152
but that's what you
would have to do here,

00:18:24.152 --> 00:18:26.187
where you create your columns.

00:18:26.187 --> 00:18:28.122
Remember the columns?

00:18:28.122 --> 00:18:29.824
All right?

00:18:29.824 --> 00:18:32.260
So you would simply make your
columns, you make your sign,

00:18:32.260 --> 00:18:37.065
your thetas, your 2 theta
theta sine squared thetas.

00:18:37.065 --> 00:18:41.035
And then you normalize,
you take the lowest one,

00:18:41.035 --> 00:18:44.305
and you normalize to 1.

00:18:44.305 --> 00:18:47.408
Clear fractions, and then
find the hkl combinations,

00:18:47.408 --> 00:18:50.645
and that's how you would
go from this to getting,

00:18:50.645 --> 00:18:53.214
for example, the planes.

00:18:53.214 --> 00:18:55.550
So those are some
examples of things

00:18:55.550 --> 00:18:59.153
I want you to be able to
do with X-rays and XRD.

00:18:59.153 --> 00:19:00.888
Let's move on.

00:19:00.888 --> 00:19:02.023
Let's go to the next topic.

00:19:02.023 --> 00:19:04.058
Let's do defects.

00:19:04.058 --> 00:19:09.263
How about this, defects,
because as we said,

00:19:09.263 --> 00:19:12.166
it's just like life,
crystals, defects

00:19:12.166 --> 00:19:13.835
are what make them interesting.

00:19:13.835 --> 00:19:14.702
It's the same.

00:19:14.702 --> 00:19:16.704
Everything, it's
all about defects,

00:19:16.704 --> 00:19:19.407
imperfections, those are
the interesting and exciting

00:19:19.407 --> 00:19:22.877
things, so we've got to know
about them in chemistry,

00:19:22.877 --> 00:19:25.747
of course.

00:19:25.747 --> 00:19:28.883
So we've talked about a bunch
of different kinds of defects.

00:19:28.883 --> 00:19:31.052
The first one that
we talked about

00:19:31.052 --> 00:19:39.127
were vacancies, vacancies, and
these could be interstitial.

00:19:41.996 --> 00:19:47.635
These fall into the category of
point defects, point defects,

00:19:47.635 --> 00:19:48.136
right?

00:19:51.039 --> 00:19:55.510
You could also have
interstitial point defects,

00:19:55.510 --> 00:20:00.048
interstitial or substitutional.

00:20:03.184 --> 00:20:05.119
Those are the different
kinds of defects we've

00:20:05.119 --> 00:20:07.355
talked about for point defects.

00:20:07.355 --> 00:20:11.492
Also, by the way, if it's an
ionic point defect, ionic point

00:20:11.492 --> 00:20:17.999
defect, you need
to make sure you

00:20:17.999 --> 00:20:25.973
have charge neutrality, charge
neutrality, plus two types.

00:20:28.776 --> 00:20:31.079
There's Frenkel, and
there's Schottky.

00:20:31.079 --> 00:20:36.050
You can think about
Schottky, you remove,

00:20:36.050 --> 00:20:39.554
but you've got to remove them
in a way that's charge-balanced.

00:20:39.554 --> 00:20:40.054
Right?

00:20:40.054 --> 00:20:42.390
So in Schottky, you remove.

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:44.358
In an ionic crystal,
you've got to keep charge.

00:20:44.358 --> 00:20:46.627
That's a new thing you've
got to keep track of.

00:20:46.627 --> 00:20:47.729
And so if we had--

00:20:47.729 --> 00:20:50.264
I'll go to this picture.

00:20:50.264 --> 00:20:52.166
Well, you should
know this picture.

00:20:52.166 --> 00:20:53.367
This is an important picture.

00:20:53.367 --> 00:20:54.469
We showed it a lot--

00:20:54.469 --> 00:20:57.538
vacancy, interstitial,
self-interstitial,

00:20:57.538 --> 00:20:58.573
substitutional.

00:20:58.573 --> 00:21:00.641
And then if it's ionic,
now all of a sudden,

00:21:00.641 --> 00:21:02.744
you've got charges in
there, and so you've just

00:21:02.744 --> 00:21:05.680
got to make sure
things remain neutral.

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:06.581
Right?

00:21:06.581 --> 00:21:10.952
In Schottky, you would remove
atoms to balance the charge,

00:21:10.952 --> 00:21:13.821
and in Frenkel,
you move an atom.

00:21:16.524 --> 00:21:19.660
Schottky remove, Frenkel move.

00:21:19.660 --> 00:21:23.731
That just occurred to me,
that that's the same word

00:21:23.731 --> 00:21:26.467
with just re in front of it.

00:21:26.467 --> 00:21:27.869
That's exciting.

00:21:27.869 --> 00:21:30.605
Well, so if I were to ask
you, for example, if I were to

00:21:30.605 --> 00:21:31.873
ask you this.

00:21:31.873 --> 00:21:37.812
You diffuse chromium into
one side of the ionic crystal

00:21:37.812 --> 00:21:41.415
alumina to make it red
and titanium on the other.

00:21:41.415 --> 00:21:44.118
Would you expect oxygen
vacancies, aluminum vacancies,

00:21:44.118 --> 00:21:48.489
or no vacancies in each case?

00:21:48.489 --> 00:21:54.962
Just follow the charge,
follow the charge.

00:21:54.962 --> 00:22:00.034
So for example, if
I had alumina, O3.

00:22:00.034 --> 00:22:03.905
Well, if I were
Lewis, if I were Lewis

00:22:03.905 --> 00:22:10.311
I would see this as 2Al3 plus.

00:22:10.311 --> 00:22:10.812
Right?

00:22:10.812 --> 00:22:14.348
This is how he saw ionic bonds.

00:22:14.348 --> 00:22:21.522
Ah, I want to make
sure this is clear.

00:22:21.522 --> 00:22:22.990
I'm going to start over.

00:22:22.990 --> 00:22:32.767
So I would have 2Al3 plus, and
then I would have a 3O2 minus.

00:22:32.767 --> 00:22:37.371
Remember, O likes to
be 2 minus, and so

00:22:37.371 --> 00:22:43.377
if I were to introduce
something like Cr2O3,

00:22:43.377 --> 00:22:56.123
well, this is 2Cr3 plus, so this
is 2Cr3 plus, and 3O2 minus.

00:22:56.123 --> 00:23:01.596
So if I substitute
aluminum with chromium,

00:23:01.596 --> 00:23:03.965
then I'm charge-balanced.

00:23:03.965 --> 00:23:04.465
Right?

00:23:04.465 --> 00:23:05.166
I'm OK.

00:23:05.166 --> 00:23:16.944
But if I substitute titanium,
TiO2 is Ti4 plus and 2O2 minus,

00:23:16.944 --> 00:23:18.746
you know this just
from the formula.

00:23:18.746 --> 00:23:22.083
Because if oxygen is 2 minus,
this thing's got to be neutral,

00:23:22.083 --> 00:23:24.719
and so that's Ti4 plus.

00:23:24.719 --> 00:23:27.355
But now, if I put a Ti
in there, and I get one

00:23:27.355 --> 00:23:32.793
in for an aluminum, I've
knocked the charge by 1.

00:23:32.793 --> 00:23:35.296
Right?

00:23:35.296 --> 00:23:43.337
So this gives us
an extra charge,

00:23:43.337 --> 00:23:45.973
and that means you've
got to create vacancies,

00:23:45.973 --> 00:23:49.343
create aluminum vacancies.

00:23:49.343 --> 00:23:51.779
It's the only way to do it.

00:23:51.779 --> 00:23:54.215
So for every three titanium
atoms that I put in,

00:23:54.215 --> 00:23:55.917
I've got to take an
aluminum one out.

00:23:55.917 --> 00:23:56.417
Right?

00:23:56.417 --> 00:23:59.320
Otherwise, I'm not going
to have charge balance,

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:00.688
not going to have
charge balance.

00:24:00.688 --> 00:24:02.023
Right?

00:24:02.023 --> 00:24:06.427
So that would be an example of
playing with an ionic crystal,

00:24:06.427 --> 00:24:09.163
but remember, we also
had stress-strain curves.

00:24:09.163 --> 00:24:14.969
Because when we talked
about line defects,

00:24:14.969 --> 00:24:18.839
we talked about how that's a way
for metals and other materials

00:24:18.839 --> 00:24:20.241
to undergo deformation.

00:24:20.241 --> 00:24:21.943
And then we said,
well, how are we

00:24:21.943 --> 00:24:26.380
going to conceptualize
these things happening,

00:24:26.380 --> 00:24:28.115
and we did it with the
stress-strain curve

00:24:28.115 --> 00:24:30.584
and the mechanical
response of materials.

00:24:30.584 --> 00:24:32.787
So we learned about
stress-strain curves

00:24:32.787 --> 00:24:42.129
and fracture points,
fracture and plastic

00:24:42.129 --> 00:24:47.001
versus elastic regimes.

00:24:47.001 --> 00:24:50.504
OK, I'll give an
example in a sec.

00:24:50.504 --> 00:24:51.005
OK?

00:24:53.841 --> 00:24:55.810
And finally, the last
thing we did with defects

00:24:55.810 --> 00:24:57.945
is we talked about
their activation.

00:24:57.945 --> 00:25:02.216
And so we know if something
is thermally activated,

00:25:02.216 --> 00:25:07.688
as are point defects, vacancies
are thermally activated,

00:25:07.688 --> 00:25:10.458
so are other types
of point defects.

00:25:10.458 --> 00:25:13.661
And we said that we need
an Arrhenius relationship,

00:25:13.661 --> 00:25:17.565
and that requires
knowledge of an activation

00:25:17.565 --> 00:25:20.267
energy for the defect to form.

00:25:20.267 --> 00:25:23.304
So that was another
really important concept

00:25:23.304 --> 00:25:24.071
that we covered.

00:25:24.071 --> 00:25:24.905
Right?

00:25:24.905 --> 00:25:29.443
So for example, here is like
a defect formation energy

00:25:29.443 --> 00:25:29.944
question.

00:25:33.180 --> 00:25:38.819
So let me go over here, stay
with this side, where I've got

00:25:38.819 --> 00:25:41.489
some defects.

00:25:41.489 --> 00:25:45.493
So one thing I
want to make clear,

00:25:45.493 --> 00:25:47.294
the fraction of vacancies.

00:25:47.294 --> 00:25:48.162
We talked about this.

00:25:48.162 --> 00:25:50.631
We talked about an
Arrhenius behavior,

00:25:50.631 --> 00:25:54.135
Arrhenius-like behavior, and
the fraction of vacancies

00:25:54.135 --> 00:25:59.106
was the number of vacancies over
the number of possible sites.

00:25:59.106 --> 00:26:00.641
That makes a lot of sense.

00:26:00.641 --> 00:26:02.376
Now, if it's
Arrhenius, then this

00:26:02.376 --> 00:26:06.614
is some constant times e to the
minus activation energy divided

00:26:06.614 --> 00:26:10.684
by KBT, where we use
the Boltzmann constant

00:26:10.684 --> 00:26:12.920
if it's per atom.

00:26:12.920 --> 00:26:14.688
Right?

00:26:14.688 --> 00:26:18.993
So per vacancy, that activation
energy would be per vacancy.

00:26:18.993 --> 00:26:23.431
But when I first introduced
this, I simply let a be 1,

00:26:23.431 --> 00:26:25.666
but a is a constant.

00:26:25.666 --> 00:26:30.805
This is a constant,
and it can be whatever.

00:26:30.805 --> 00:26:31.572
It's a constant.

00:26:31.572 --> 00:26:36.243
It's called the
anthropic factor,

00:26:36.243 --> 00:26:39.547
and it's a constant related
to as you would have

00:26:39.547 --> 00:26:42.149
in any Arrhenius-like behavior.

00:26:42.149 --> 00:26:43.384
Right?

00:26:43.384 --> 00:26:46.053
Remember how it's like
a frequency factor,

00:26:46.053 --> 00:26:49.457
this is related to all
sorts of complexities

00:26:49.457 --> 00:26:52.827
that we don't need to know about
related to how vacancies form.

00:26:52.827 --> 00:26:54.962
It's a constant that's in
there, and I let it be 1,

00:26:54.962 --> 00:26:57.431
but I want to make sure
we put it in explicitly.

00:27:01.902 --> 00:27:04.672
This n is the number
of possible sites.

00:27:04.672 --> 00:27:05.172
Right?

00:27:05.172 --> 00:27:09.710
So maybe it's the number of
lattice sites in the crystal,

00:27:09.710 --> 00:27:11.011
number of possible sites.

00:27:11.011 --> 00:27:14.081
But you might get
this could be related

00:27:14.081 --> 00:27:23.457
to, for example, or to or
from, things like the grams

00:27:23.457 --> 00:27:29.530
that you have of something
or maybe the volume

00:27:29.530 --> 00:27:30.564
or the moles, et cetera.

00:27:30.564 --> 00:27:31.065
Right?

00:27:31.065 --> 00:27:35.002
So these are the kinds
of problems we've done.

00:27:35.002 --> 00:27:39.940
That gives you a sense of
how many sites did you have.

00:27:39.940 --> 00:27:40.908
Right?

00:27:40.908 --> 00:27:43.511
It could be per
centimeter cubed.

00:27:43.511 --> 00:27:45.746
It could be per moles.

00:27:45.746 --> 00:27:47.381
You go back and forth.

00:27:47.381 --> 00:27:49.783
How much stuff do I have?

00:27:49.783 --> 00:27:52.153
It's that kind of
back and forth.

00:27:52.153 --> 00:27:56.157
If I had a problem like this,
then I know I can set it up,

00:27:56.157 --> 00:27:58.993
because I've got
two temperatures.

00:27:58.993 --> 00:28:01.795
You see the key here,
the concentration of 10

00:28:01.795 --> 00:28:05.099
to the cubed per
centimeter cubed,

00:28:05.099 --> 00:28:06.433
so don't let that throw you off.

00:28:06.433 --> 00:28:10.905
It's simply two equations,
where a bunch of stuff cancels.

00:28:10.905 --> 00:28:11.906
Right?

00:28:11.906 --> 00:28:16.810
So the fraction
of vacancies, fv1,

00:28:16.810 --> 00:28:21.515
would be 10 to the
3rd divided by fv2,

00:28:21.515 --> 00:28:26.053
and that's going to be 10 to
the 3rd over 10 to the 16th,

00:28:26.053 --> 00:28:30.357
because the number
of stuff cancels.

00:28:30.357 --> 00:28:30.858
Right?

00:28:30.858 --> 00:28:32.326
I didn't change that.

00:28:32.326 --> 00:28:34.662
But this pre-factor cancels too.

00:28:34.662 --> 00:28:43.003
You can put it there, A times
e to the minus ea divided by KB

00:28:43.003 --> 00:28:46.774
times 300 Kelvin--
remember, so it was Kelvin--

00:28:46.774 --> 00:28:55.683
divided by A times to the
minus ea divided by KB times

00:28:55.683 --> 00:28:59.186
the other temperature,
900 Kelvin.

00:28:59.186 --> 00:28:59.920
Right?

00:28:59.920 --> 00:29:04.692
And so those cancel,
the stuff related to n

00:29:04.692 --> 00:29:07.761
cancels, and I can just take
the number of vacancies.

00:29:07.761 --> 00:29:08.429
Right?

00:29:08.429 --> 00:29:10.197
So that's what's nice about
having something like this.

00:29:10.197 --> 00:29:12.499
You have two temperatures
and a whole bunch of stuff

00:29:12.499 --> 00:29:17.605
can cancel, and you can figure
out then the activation energy

00:29:17.605 --> 00:29:19.707
which doesn't change.

00:29:19.707 --> 00:29:23.510
The activation energy is simply
the energy that you need,

00:29:23.510 --> 00:29:29.250
so you can now solve for ea,
and I think you get something

00:29:29.250 --> 00:29:31.518
like 2.3 electron volts.

00:29:31.518 --> 00:29:33.921
Remember that you could
also have plotted it.

00:29:33.921 --> 00:29:36.457
You'd know that
in this equation,

00:29:36.457 --> 00:29:40.261
in the Arrhenius relationship,
if you take the log,

00:29:40.261 --> 00:29:45.065
then you have the minus
1 over T relationship.

00:29:45.065 --> 00:29:46.734
So remember that?

00:29:46.734 --> 00:29:50.504
So if you take the
T versus the log,

00:29:50.504 --> 00:29:52.606
then you're going to get that.

00:29:52.606 --> 00:29:54.875
Right?

00:29:54.875 --> 00:29:57.044
We like plots, when we
think about Arrhenius,

00:29:57.044 --> 00:29:58.178
because it makes it easier.

00:29:58.178 --> 00:30:01.415
We can make it linear,
where the slope of this

00:30:01.415 --> 00:30:09.323
is minus ea, the slope, minus
ea over KB, in this case.

00:30:09.323 --> 00:30:09.823
Right?

00:30:09.823 --> 00:30:12.026
So we talked about these
are all kinds of things

00:30:12.026 --> 00:30:13.060
that we talked about.

00:30:13.060 --> 00:30:14.862
And finally, getting
to this stress-strain,

00:30:14.862 --> 00:30:16.697
what if we looked at this?

00:30:16.697 --> 00:30:20.000
This is the stress-strain,
so now we're here.

00:30:20.000 --> 00:30:21.135
We just did this.

00:30:21.135 --> 00:30:22.036
We talked about that.

00:30:22.036 --> 00:30:24.405
We talked about this,
so stress-strain.

00:30:24.405 --> 00:30:28.809
So if you were to be given
plots like this, you should know

00:30:28.809 --> 00:30:30.344
which one is most
brittle, which one

00:30:30.344 --> 00:30:33.280
is the stiffest, the
most resistant to

00:30:33.280 --> 00:30:39.119
changing elastically, and
which one is the most ductile?

00:30:39.119 --> 00:30:41.255
You can see that
the stiffest one

00:30:41.255 --> 00:30:45.659
is going to be the one
that's the most resistant to

00:30:45.659 --> 00:30:46.860
elastic deformation.

00:30:46.860 --> 00:30:49.330
That's that linear
part, that first part.

00:30:49.330 --> 00:30:51.532
So there, it's
going to be material

00:30:51.532 --> 00:30:56.236
A. It's going to be A, because
it's got the highest slope.

00:30:56.236 --> 00:30:57.304
Right?

00:30:57.304 --> 00:30:58.839
OK.

00:30:58.839 --> 00:30:59.973
How about the most brittle?

00:30:59.973 --> 00:31:03.444
Well, that's the one
that's going to break.

00:31:03.444 --> 00:31:05.045
So it sort of looks like--

00:31:05.045 --> 00:31:08.449
without deforming, without being
able to undergo defamation,

00:31:08.449 --> 00:31:10.417
that's brittleness.

00:31:10.417 --> 00:31:12.786
It's going to crack
before doing anything.

00:31:12.786 --> 00:31:13.921
So that's B, right?

00:31:13.921 --> 00:31:15.389
That's B.

00:31:15.389 --> 00:31:18.325
And then the most ductile will
be the one that has the most

00:31:18.325 --> 00:31:21.562
plastic deformation, because
that's what ductility is,

00:31:21.562 --> 00:31:25.165
so that's going to be C. OK?

00:31:25.165 --> 00:31:26.667
So remember
stress-strain curves.

00:31:26.667 --> 00:31:30.270
You've got this elastic
part here, that's linear,

00:31:30.270 --> 00:31:34.274
and then if it's a material--
not all materials have

00:31:34.274 --> 00:31:37.010
plastic defamation.

00:31:37.010 --> 00:31:38.912
Some are going to
break before they

00:31:38.912 --> 00:31:42.950
start deforming plastically, but
if it can deform plastically,

00:31:42.950 --> 00:31:45.719
then it's going to
have that yield point.

00:31:45.719 --> 00:31:46.220
Right?

00:31:46.220 --> 00:31:49.156
The yield stress, where then
it starts becoming non-linear,

00:31:49.156 --> 00:31:54.161
and then finally, it
fractures somewhere else.

00:31:54.161 --> 00:31:54.661
OK?

00:31:54.661 --> 00:31:56.930
So those are
stress-strain curves.

00:31:56.930 --> 00:32:00.334
OK, let's move on.

00:32:00.334 --> 00:32:07.941
Now, oh, with the next topic--

00:32:07.941 --> 00:32:11.945
oh boy, I'm going to
run out of real estate--

00:32:11.945 --> 00:32:21.054
we've got glasses, and here,
the most important thing

00:32:21.054 --> 00:32:23.791
to know about is the curve.

00:32:23.791 --> 00:32:24.958
That's how it all started.

00:32:24.958 --> 00:32:25.459
Right?

00:32:25.459 --> 00:32:26.860
I'm calling it The Curve.

00:32:26.860 --> 00:32:30.931
Let's go all caps,
because it's The Curve,

00:32:30.931 --> 00:32:34.101
and what I mean by The
Curve is the volume

00:32:34.101 --> 00:32:40.474
per mole versus temperature.

00:32:40.474 --> 00:32:50.551
We also talked about the
effects of cooling rate,

00:32:50.551 --> 00:32:59.460
and we also talked about
the effects of modifiers,

00:32:59.460 --> 00:33:05.933
and in particular how the
modifier changes the viscosity

00:33:05.933 --> 00:33:08.402
because it cuts the pasta.

00:33:08.402 --> 00:33:14.808
OK, so but the framing of these
concepts was in The Curve,

00:33:14.808 --> 00:33:15.642
and that's this one.

00:33:15.642 --> 00:33:17.077
Oh, I have a picture of it.

00:33:17.077 --> 00:33:18.745
There it is.

00:33:18.745 --> 00:33:21.114
OK, they did specific--
we do volume per mole

00:33:21.114 --> 00:33:23.584
is how we're looking at it in
this class, so volume per mole

00:33:23.584 --> 00:33:25.853
versus temperature.

00:33:25.853 --> 00:33:30.824
Now remember, if it's a
crystal, where am I going to go?

00:33:30.824 --> 00:33:32.392
Let's go here.

00:33:32.392 --> 00:33:37.464
If it's a crystal, then you've
got one melting point that

00:33:37.464 --> 00:33:41.735
only depends on the material.

00:33:41.735 --> 00:33:48.175
It only depends on the material,
so that melting point is Tm,

00:33:48.175 --> 00:33:51.645
and it's going to have a
big volume change as it goes

00:33:51.645 --> 00:33:55.215
from liquid to crystal solid.

00:33:55.215 --> 00:33:56.350
OK?

00:33:56.350 --> 00:34:01.722
And so for example, I'm just
basically replotting it,

00:34:01.722 --> 00:34:04.591
but you would have something
like this and something

00:34:04.591 --> 00:34:06.860
like this and
something like this,

00:34:06.860 --> 00:34:10.230
and that's your melting point.

00:34:10.230 --> 00:34:13.166
This would be the liquid,
and this would be the solid,

00:34:13.166 --> 00:34:15.636
and you know that, if it's
going to undergo this big volume

00:34:15.636 --> 00:34:16.603
change.

00:34:16.603 --> 00:34:19.840
Then, it's the melting
point and it's a crystal.

00:34:19.840 --> 00:34:22.576
But then we say, well,
but if it can't find--

00:34:22.576 --> 00:34:26.713
by crystal I mean in opposition
to glass, which we know

00:34:26.713 --> 00:34:28.482
doesn't just mean your windows.

00:34:28.482 --> 00:34:32.351
It means any amorphous material,
meaning it's not crystalline.

00:34:32.351 --> 00:34:34.888
It didn't find the
lattice structure.

00:34:34.888 --> 00:34:37.724
Remember those three
reasons, the musical chairs?

00:34:37.724 --> 00:34:39.560
Whatever you're trying
to crystallize, atoms

00:34:39.560 --> 00:34:43.563
or molecules, they weren't able
to find all the lattice sites,

00:34:43.563 --> 00:34:46.199
not even withstanding
some defects here.

00:34:46.199 --> 00:34:49.303
They simply couldn't do
it, and so it's amorphous,

00:34:49.303 --> 00:34:50.971
and it's a glass.

00:34:50.971 --> 00:34:54.206
And in that case,
what happens is it

00:34:54.206 --> 00:35:02.583
super cools and then
becomes a solid at something

00:35:02.583 --> 00:35:04.217
called the glass
transition temperature.

00:35:04.217 --> 00:35:08.422
Which unlike the crystal
can have different values,

00:35:08.422 --> 00:35:11.959
and those values can depend on
things like the cooling rate.

00:35:11.959 --> 00:35:17.631
So if I cool it slower,
if I cool it slower--

00:35:17.631 --> 00:35:24.037
remember this is faster,
faster, slower, and please,

00:35:24.037 --> 00:35:26.506
do not confuse time
with this axis.

00:35:26.506 --> 00:35:28.175
This is just temperature.

00:35:28.175 --> 00:35:30.143
So we can use the same--

00:35:30.143 --> 00:35:35.082
this is volume per mole-- we can
use the same The Plot to think

00:35:35.082 --> 00:35:37.250
about modifiers as well.

00:35:37.250 --> 00:35:41.254
Because it's not-- this is
just one plot, one curve

00:35:41.254 --> 00:35:42.255
and another.

00:35:42.255 --> 00:35:44.124
This happened to
be cooled slower,

00:35:44.124 --> 00:35:46.960
so it was able to
super cool more,

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:49.129
before it locked
in the disorder,

00:35:49.129 --> 00:35:50.764
and the other one's faster.

00:35:50.764 --> 00:35:53.600
You could also imagine
if you cut the pasta,

00:35:53.600 --> 00:35:56.670
you change the viscosity.

00:35:56.670 --> 00:35:58.538
And so now you're
changing something else

00:35:58.538 --> 00:36:00.674
about the glass using
chemistry, and you're

00:36:00.674 --> 00:36:04.111
allowing it to find those
lattice sites more easily.

00:36:04.111 --> 00:36:06.146
You're allowing it to try
to become crystalline,

00:36:06.146 --> 00:36:08.882
to try to close pack
just a little bit more.

00:36:08.882 --> 00:36:11.885
So you can also get this,
by cutting the pasta you

00:36:11.885 --> 00:36:15.288
can get these kinds of changes.

00:36:15.288 --> 00:36:19.292
So those are the things we
talked about with glass.

00:36:19.292 --> 00:36:26.033
Now, next, we talked
about reactions.

00:36:26.033 --> 00:36:26.533
OK.

00:36:34.274 --> 00:36:41.715
What do we need to know about
reactions, reaction rates?

00:36:41.715 --> 00:36:49.222
So we want to know the order
of some general reaction,

00:36:49.222 --> 00:36:51.892
like aA plus bB--

00:36:51.892 --> 00:36:55.629
remember this--
goes to cC plus dD.

00:36:58.832 --> 00:37:06.907
And we want to know
the rate from--

00:37:06.907 --> 00:37:09.609
basically, it's the
mass conservation,

00:37:09.609 --> 00:37:13.046
so from the coefficients.

00:37:13.046 --> 00:37:15.415
OK, so that would
be, for example,

00:37:15.415 --> 00:37:19.519
like minus 1 over a, d(A), dt--

00:37:19.519 --> 00:37:25.959
that's a rate-- equals minus
1 over b d(B), dt, et cetera.

00:37:30.097 --> 00:37:32.165
Now, those come from
the coefficients,

00:37:32.165 --> 00:37:38.071
but the rate law does not,
the rate law does not.

00:37:38.071 --> 00:37:40.273
So these are simply
instantaneous rates,

00:37:40.273 --> 00:37:43.110
but now I say, no, I
want a rate law that

00:37:43.110 --> 00:37:50.083
gives me the dependence on these
concentrations at any time.

00:37:53.854 --> 00:38:00.193
So the rate law would
be, for example,

00:38:00.193 --> 00:38:09.336
like rate equals k times
A to the m, B to the n.

00:38:09.336 --> 00:38:11.671
Now, here's the thing.

00:38:11.671 --> 00:38:16.243
These come from experiments.

00:38:21.481 --> 00:38:24.284
Very important
distinction, right?

00:38:24.284 --> 00:38:27.120
Those come from
experiments, and so you

00:38:27.120 --> 00:38:29.656
could get the rate with
respect to A which would be--

00:38:29.656 --> 00:38:30.257
I'm sorry.

00:38:30.257 --> 00:38:33.593
The order with respect
to A would be m,

00:38:33.593 --> 00:38:35.762
the order with respect
to B would be n,

00:38:35.762 --> 00:38:38.165
and the overall
order of the reaction

00:38:38.165 --> 00:38:41.268
is m plus n, the overall order.

00:38:44.604 --> 00:38:48.942
And then, we had one more
item here of conceptually

00:38:48.942 --> 00:38:52.212
what I want you to know about,
which is that the rate also

00:38:52.212 --> 00:38:54.681
depends on temperature.

00:38:54.681 --> 00:38:59.686
Sorry, the rate
constant, rate constant

00:38:59.686 --> 00:39:02.756
depends on temperature.

00:39:02.756 --> 00:39:04.991
So if I were to now
say, well, OK, I

00:39:04.991 --> 00:39:07.294
want to know something about
the order of a reaction,

00:39:07.294 --> 00:39:11.064
you might get a table like
this, and we did this in class.

00:39:11.064 --> 00:39:12.833
You might get a
table just like this.

00:39:12.833 --> 00:39:13.767
Here's a reaction.

00:39:13.767 --> 00:39:18.405
It's W and X and Y,
and it goes to Z.

00:39:18.405 --> 00:39:21.942
Don't let the fact that you have
three reactants throw you off.

00:39:21.942 --> 00:39:23.643
You've got a certain
number of reactants,

00:39:23.643 --> 00:39:27.714
they've got their coefficients,
and they go to some product.

00:39:27.714 --> 00:39:30.550
And now, you're given
experimental data, so

00:39:30.550 --> 00:39:33.620
from this data, you
can get the order

00:39:33.620 --> 00:39:36.823
of the reaction with respect
to each one of these.

00:39:36.823 --> 00:39:37.757
Right?

00:39:37.757 --> 00:39:40.794
And that would then give you,
of course, the overall order.

00:39:40.794 --> 00:39:41.494
So let's see.

00:39:41.494 --> 00:39:47.500
If we were to look at that
data, then what would we get?

00:39:47.500 --> 00:39:54.808
So what you want to do in
these kinds of situations is,

00:39:54.808 --> 00:39:57.210
first of all, don't panic.

00:39:57.210 --> 00:39:59.813
By the way, on an exam,
in general, don't panic,

00:39:59.813 --> 00:40:02.015
and if you have a question,
you can ask a question,

00:40:02.015 --> 00:40:04.985
but also show us what you know.

00:40:04.985 --> 00:40:07.888
We will give you-- as you
I hope have seen already--

00:40:07.888 --> 00:40:10.657
we will give you as much
credit as we can for sharing

00:40:10.657 --> 00:40:11.258
what you know.

00:40:11.258 --> 00:40:14.794
You don't have to get everything
right to get a lot of points.

00:40:14.794 --> 00:40:17.931
Please share your knowledge.

00:40:17.931 --> 00:40:19.900
So in this case, what
would the knowledge be?

00:40:19.900 --> 00:40:21.868
Well, it would be that
I want to compare stuff,

00:40:21.868 --> 00:40:23.370
like trial 1 and 2.

00:40:23.370 --> 00:40:27.974
And if I do that, you see,
OK, trial 1, trial 2, then two

00:40:27.974 --> 00:40:31.111
of them stay constant,
but W doubles.

00:40:31.111 --> 00:40:32.078
OK?

00:40:32.078 --> 00:40:44.090
So comparing 1 versus 2, you
got W goes to 2 two times,

00:40:44.090 --> 00:40:48.662
but the rate is
same, so you know

00:40:48.662 --> 00:40:58.471
the order is 0 with respect to
W. But if I compare 1 versus 3,

00:40:58.471 --> 00:41:04.744
then you know that,
let's see, x goes to 3x

00:41:04.744 --> 00:41:14.487
and the rate goes to 3x,
and so the order is 1.

00:41:14.487 --> 00:41:16.623
And then finally, for Y--

00:41:16.623 --> 00:41:18.625
I will just jump to it--

00:41:18.625 --> 00:41:25.398
the order is 2, and you can
do that just by find one

00:41:25.398 --> 00:41:27.634
where only Y changes.

00:41:27.634 --> 00:41:30.303
You don't have to
necessarily find one

00:41:30.303 --> 00:41:31.638
where just one changes.

00:41:31.638 --> 00:41:33.640
Right?

00:41:33.640 --> 00:41:37.777
Because if you knew
one is first order--

00:41:37.777 --> 00:41:40.280
if you knew one, and then you
had another one, and they both

00:41:40.280 --> 00:41:40.747
changed.

00:41:40.747 --> 00:41:42.148
But maybe the rate
didn't change,

00:41:42.148 --> 00:41:44.551
then you know they must have
both the same order, if they

00:41:44.551 --> 00:41:46.186
change in opposite ways.

00:41:46.186 --> 00:41:46.853
Right?

00:41:46.853 --> 00:41:48.621
So you can get into
this in different ways,

00:41:48.621 --> 00:41:52.926
just looking at these
changes from one to another

00:41:52.926 --> 00:41:55.328
and thinking about
what must happen

00:41:55.328 --> 00:41:56.963
in terms of the
rate law, in terms

00:41:56.963 --> 00:41:59.032
of what you wrote out here.

00:42:02.302 --> 00:42:07.707
But you could also ask
questions like with reactions,

00:42:07.707 --> 00:42:09.242
OK, this is first order.

00:42:09.242 --> 00:42:11.011
What's the dependence
of the concentration

00:42:11.011 --> 00:42:13.646
over time as a function
of the rate constant?

00:42:13.646 --> 00:42:18.685
Well, that just goes back to our
understanding of the integrated

00:42:18.685 --> 00:42:19.919
rate equation.

00:42:19.919 --> 00:42:20.653
Right?

00:42:20.653 --> 00:42:26.993
And so let's see, oh
boy, now I need to find--

00:42:26.993 --> 00:42:28.395
I'm going to go--

00:42:28.395 --> 00:42:30.130
I hate to do this, XRD.

00:42:30.130 --> 00:42:38.705
I hate to do this, but
sometimes being first carries

00:42:38.705 --> 00:42:39.506
a consequence.

00:42:42.742 --> 00:42:44.377
OK.

00:42:44.377 --> 00:42:45.612
So in this case--

00:42:45.612 --> 00:42:47.514
first of all, by the
way, you look at reaction

00:42:47.514 --> 00:42:49.749
like that, you're like, well
OK, from the coefficients

00:42:49.749 --> 00:42:53.420
and the mass balance stuff, you
know that the change in oxygen,

00:42:53.420 --> 00:42:55.622
for example, with
time, the instantaneous

00:42:55.622 --> 00:43:03.396
rate would equal minus the
change in MO2 with time.

00:43:03.396 --> 00:43:05.098
Well, that's not what
the question asked.

00:43:05.098 --> 00:43:07.567
The question says
it's first order.

00:43:07.567 --> 00:43:15.775
So that's enough, because
this means that if I integrate

00:43:15.775 --> 00:43:20.080
the rate law then-- first of
all, if it's first order--

00:43:20.080 --> 00:43:25.318
then d of MO2 with
respect to time

00:43:25.318 --> 00:43:31.558
must equal some constant
times the concentration of MO2

00:43:31.558 --> 00:43:35.462
to the 1, first order.

00:43:35.462 --> 00:43:37.597
And so now, if I
integrate this, I

00:43:37.597 --> 00:43:40.733
take this concentration over
here, I put the dt over there.

00:43:40.733 --> 00:43:41.568
You integrate it.

00:43:41.568 --> 00:43:43.236
You've got all the
integrated rate laws.

00:43:43.236 --> 00:43:44.804
We've gone through this already.

00:43:44.804 --> 00:43:48.641
You know that you
get that the log--

00:43:48.641 --> 00:43:50.310
oh, we do logs--

00:43:50.310 --> 00:43:58.251
are this MO2 equals the log
of some starting concentration

00:43:58.251 --> 00:44:00.086
minus Kt.

00:44:00.086 --> 00:44:02.088
OK?

00:44:02.088 --> 00:44:02.856
Why do we do that?

00:44:02.856 --> 00:44:05.325
Because we like plotting
things, linear things.

00:44:05.325 --> 00:44:08.361
We love linear.

00:44:08.361 --> 00:44:10.263
So remember, we did this.

00:44:10.263 --> 00:44:12.165
We went from these
integrated rate

00:44:12.165 --> 00:44:14.400
laws to plots and
back and forth,

00:44:14.400 --> 00:44:17.036
and so that's also
very important.

