00:45:23.603 --> 00:45:27.173
If I add BaCl2 to a
saturated solution of BaSO4,

00:45:27.173 --> 00:45:31.110
why does it cause precipitation?

00:45:31.110 --> 00:45:32.779
Answer-- because
of the common ion

00:45:32.779 --> 00:45:36.216
effect, but you could
write these out.

00:45:36.216 --> 00:45:39.552
So you write out your
saturated solution,

00:45:39.552 --> 00:45:43.122
so you write out
your BaSO4, and you

00:45:43.122 --> 00:45:54.033
see that BaSO4 solid will
go to Ba2 plus in solution

00:45:54.033 --> 00:45:57.937
plus SO4 2 minus in solution.

00:45:57.937 --> 00:46:00.773
And so the common
ion effect tells you

00:46:00.773 --> 00:46:04.744
that if I now add something else
that has something in common

00:46:04.744 --> 00:46:07.981
with one of these, that
gives me an ion in common,

00:46:07.981 --> 00:46:09.482
then because of Le
Chatelier, you're

00:46:09.482 --> 00:46:12.518
going to drive the
reaction the other way.

00:46:12.518 --> 00:46:14.954
That precipitates, because
it forms a solid BaSO4.

00:46:14.954 --> 00:46:15.888
So I've just done that.

00:46:15.888 --> 00:46:16.389
Right?

