00:47:37.103 --> 00:47:40.840
And so for example, if you're
given a solution-- this is one,

00:47:40.840 --> 00:47:44.911
just a simple
example of an acid--

00:47:44.911 --> 00:47:46.913
and it's HOCN, and
you're given the acid.

00:47:46.913 --> 00:47:50.116
And you know the pH,
so you're given that.

00:47:50.116 --> 00:47:56.155
Then the pH from that
information you can get that

00:47:56.155 --> 00:48:00.460
the concentration of H plus--
which is also a H3O plus--

00:48:00.460 --> 00:48:06.265
is 1.7 times 10 to the
minus 3 moles per liter.

00:48:06.265 --> 00:48:12.138
That's from the pH,
and if you have that,

00:48:12.138 --> 00:48:17.243
then you can go to the
Ka definition, which

00:48:17.243 --> 00:48:26.386
is H plus concentration OCN
minus divided by the acid HOCN

00:48:26.386 --> 00:48:28.921
concentration, and
you've got it all.

00:48:28.921 --> 00:48:32.759
Because if you're right the
equilibrium equation, which

00:48:32.759 --> 00:48:38.331
is what Ka describes, the
equilibrium of this acid

00:48:38.331 --> 00:48:42.368
dissociating into
ions, dissociating

00:48:42.368 --> 00:48:45.338
into H plus and OCN minus,
and so you can get Ka.

